Great! Here is the full step by step command sequence for the demo.

**Before the demo — prep work (you do this, not the employees)**

SSH into the DPU ARM:
```bash
ssh ubuntu@192.168.100.2  # your DPU ARM IP
```

Set up hugepages:
```bash
echo 1024 > /sys/kernel/mm/hugepages/hugepages-2048kB/nr_hugepages
```

Persist it across reboots:
```bash
echo "vm.nr_hugepages = 1024" > /etc/sysctl.d/99-hugepages.conf
```

Start the traffic generator on the host x86 side so there is something to show dropping. From the host:
```bash
ping -f 192.168.1.1  # flood ping from the "attacker" IP
```

Or better with iperf:
```bash
iperf3 -c <target_ip> -t 3600  # continuous traffic
```

Open htop on the host in a separate terminal so CPU usage is visible:
```bash
htop
```

---

**Step 1 — Launch testpmd (employees do this)**

On the DPU ARM:
```bash
/opt/mellanox/dpdk/bin/dpdk-testpmd -- --total-num-mbufs=100000 --flow-isolate-all -i
```

Wait for it to initialize. You will see the port summary. Confirm ports are up:
```
testpmd> show port summary all
```

Identify which port is the host-facing representor. It will be `pf0hpf` or `pf0vf0` — note its port number, let us say it is port 1.

---

**Step 2 — Create the firewall rule (employees do this)**

```
testpmd> flow create 1 ingress transfer pattern eth / ipv4 src is 192.168.1.100 / end actions drop / count / end
```

You should see:
```
Flow rule #0 created
```

That means the rule is now sitting in hardware.

---

**Step 3 — Prove it is working**

Check the hit counter:
```
testpmd> flow query 1 0 count
```

You will see `hits` increasing every time you run it. That number is packets being killed in hardware.

Check the CPU on the host in your htop window — usage for that traffic should have dropped significantly or vanished.

---

**Step 4 — Show it is really in hardware (the wow moment)**

Open a second terminal on the DPU ARM and run:
```bash
git clone https://github.com/Mellanox/mlx_steering_dump
./mlx_steering_dump/mlx_steering_dump_parser.py -p `pidof dpdk-testpmd` -f /tmp/dump
```

They will see their attacker IP in the raw hardware table output. That is the moment you say "you just programmed a silicon chip with one line of text."

---

**Step 5 — Clean up and show the reverse**

Delete the rule and watch traffic come back:
```
testpmd> flow destroy 1 rule 0
```

CPU usage on htop jumps back up. Hits stop counting. Everything is back to normal. That before and after is the clearest possible demonstration of what a DPU does.

---

**One thing to confirm on your setup before the demo day:**

Run this first to make sure your port numbering matches:
```
testpmd> show port summary all
```

Cross reference the MAC addresses and names against the table in the article to confirm which port number is your host-facing port. The flow rule has to go on the right port or it simply will not match any traffic.
